// 2026-07-15: Convert Briev string to C-style null-terminated string.
// Briev string format: [8-byte length (i64)][UTF-8 data].
// C string format: [data][null byte].
// Allocates via Malloc# — caller must free.
// 2026-08-23: migrated to typed String parameters (the old Int-handle
// string ABI predates the string unification).

// 2026-10-12 (daily-driver quick wins, os/fs): Copy#/Fill# take INTEGER
// addresses (the module-wide convention — every walker passes `(x as Data)
// as Int`). Passing the String operand directly hit an ambiguity the type
// system cannot express: a String register is i64-valued when it came from
// state/a load but ptr-valued when it is a function parameter, and
// Copy#'s inttoptr assumed i64. Route both operands through explicit Int
// casts so the battle-tested cast-emitter paths decide the representation.
// The Int is the String HEADER address ([len][bytes]) — the bytes live at
// +8 (cli.bv's Load#(key, 8) len-read proves the offset).
// First real emitter user: string_c.bv has no prelude import, so this defn
// never emitted before os/fs.bv.
//
// Returns the address as an Int (the module-wide address convention) — a
// Ptr-typed RETURN is physically ptr-valued at the call site while every
// other Ptr register is i64-valued (Malloc# ptrtoints at emission), and the
// two representations are indistinguishable to the cast emitters.
defn to_c_string(s: String) -> Int {
    let len: Int = s .^Length;
    let cstr: Ptr<Bit<8>> = Malloc#(len + 1);
    Copy#(cstr, (s as Data) as Int + 8, len);
    Fill#(cstr as Int + len, 0, 1);
    term cstr as Int;
};
